# Commit-time half of the three-layer ban (D-13).
#
# This is the layer that matters. A large share of commits on this project are
# authored by Claude sessions, and D-02 leaves no CI pipeline to hang a gate on,
# so this hook is the only thing standing between a forbidden token and
# permanent history. Version-controlled under .husky/ rather than hand-placed in
# .git/hooks/, because the latter is not tracked and a fresh clone would
# silently lose the protection -- which is the exact failure this exists to
# prevent. `npm install` reinstalls it via the `prepare` script.
#
# Two checks only. It runs on every commit, and a slow hook is a hook someone
# disables. No test suite, no typecheck -- those have their own gate.

# Abort on the first failing command. NOT redundant, and not style: without it
# this script exits with the status of its LAST command, which is the
# conditional at the bottom -- and that succeeds whenever grep finds nothing. A
# non-zero exit from the scan below would be printed and then discarded, and the
# commit would proceed. The gate previously worked only because husky's
# generated wrapper .husky/_/h invokes this file as `sh -e`: a line in a
# gitignored file this hook does not mention, which vanishes the moment husky is
# removed, downgraded, swapped for another runner, core.hooksPath is repointed,
# or someone runs `sh .husky/pre-commit` by hand to test it.
set -e

# 1. The forbidden-token scan. Same module test/forbidden-tokens.test.ts
#    imports, so the two layers cannot disagree about what is banned. It prints
#    each violation as `file:line — why` and exits non-zero.
node scripts/forbidden-tokens.mjs

# 2. The local secrets file. .gitignore already covers it, but `git add -f`
#    defeats that on its own, and this file carries a live Apple app-specific
#    password that would be permanent the moment it lands in history.
#    `.dev.vars.example` is deliberately not matched -- it holds no values.
if git diff --cached --name-only --diff-filter=ACMR | grep -qE '(^|/)\.dev\.vars$'; then
	echo "" >&2
	echo "Refusing the commit: .dev.vars is staged." >&2
	echo "" >&2
	echo "  That file holds a live Apple app-specific password. Once committed it is" >&2
	echo "  in history permanently, and rotating the credential is the only remedy." >&2
	echo "  Unstage it with: git restore --staged .dev.vars" >&2
	echo "" >&2
	exit 1
fi
